1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,623
Imagine two neurons whose activity settles to a steady pattern.

2
00:00:04,623 --> 00:00:10,861
Give the pattern a small nudge. If it returns, the model can hold that pattern like a

3
00:00:10,861 --> 00:00:15,264
memory. Can you predict these memories just from the wiring?

4
00:00:15,633 --> 00:00:19,575
A firing rate is a nonnegative number describing activity.

5
00:00:19,575 --> 00:00:24,537
We are studying a mathematical network model, not measuring a real brain.

6
00:00:24,537 --> 00:00:30,246
Its rate changes by balancing natural decay against input from the other neurons and

7
00:00:30,246 --> 00:00:31,401
a constant drive.

8
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:36,524
The threshold is simple. If the total input is positive, keep it.

9
00:00:36,524 --> 00:00:39,286
If it is negative, replace it by zero.

10
00:00:39,286 --> 00:00:44,664
Draw the output against input: flat on the left, then rising on the right.

11
00:00:45,033 --> 00:00:50,889
At the settings used here, an arrow means weaker inhibition, with weight minus three

12
00:00:50,889 --> 00:00:55,142
quarters. Without an arrow, the weight is minus three halves.

13
00:00:55,142 --> 00:01:01,137
Both interactions suppress activity. An arrow is not a positive excitatory connection.

14
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:04,322
Take two neurons with arrows both ways.

15
00:01:04,322 --> 00:01:09,613
If both fire at rate x, each receives input one minus three quarters of x.

16
00:01:09,613 --> 00:01:15,189
At equilibrium, the firing rate equals that input, so the rate stops changing.

17
00:01:15,567 --> 00:01:18,321
Move the three quarters of x to the left.

18
00:01:18,321 --> 00:01:20,940
One and three quarters of x equals one.

19
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:23,560
Multiply by four, then divide by seven.

20
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:26,583
Each neuron settles at exactly four sevenths.

21
00:01:26,967 --> 00:01:31,697
Now raise the first rate a little. It suppresses the second more strongly.

22
00:01:31,697 --> 00:01:35,086
That drop then releases some inhibition on the first.

23
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:40,520
This is positive feedback through two negative connections, even though neither arrow

24
00:01:40,520 --> 00:01:41,415
is excitatory.

25
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,464
But feedback does not automatically win.

26
00:01:44,464 --> 00:01:48,661
Here the natural decay is strong enough to damp the difference.

27
00:01:48,661 --> 00:01:52,857
One rate falls while the other rises back toward four sevenths.

28
00:01:52,857 --> 00:01:56,920
Returning after sufficiently small nudges is local stability.

29
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:59,892
The active neurons are called the support.

30
00:01:59,892 --> 00:02:04,212
A clique is a group with arrows in both directions between every pair.

31
00:02:04,212 --> 00:02:08,964
Our two-neuron example is a clique. Here is the same idea with three neurons.

32
00:02:09,333 --> 00:02:13,714
A target is an outside neuron receiving arrows from the whole group.

33
00:02:13,714 --> 00:02:18,416
Remove one of those arrows and that outside neuron is no longer a target.

34
00:02:18,416 --> 00:02:22,989
A clique is target-free when no outside neuron receives from all of it.

35
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:28,699
Curto, Geneson and Morrison conjectured that only target-free cliques could support stable

36
00:02:28,699 --> 00:02:32,254
memories. The direction saying these cliques work is proved.

37
00:02:32,254 --> 00:02:37,527
The converse is the vulnerable part: could a stable pattern use a group missing an arrow?

38
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:44,259
We will examine an eleven-neuron example at the standard settings: epsilon one quarter,

39
00:02:44,259 --> 00:02:50,692
delta one half, and drive one. All eleven neurons stay active and stable, although their

40
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:52,300
group is not a clique.

41
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:56,392
Start with every possible arrow between different neurons.

42
00:02:56,392 --> 00:03:00,210
There are a hundred and ten. Delete these twenty-one arrows.

43
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:05,557
The red dashed arrows show absences. Every connection still acts; the missing arrows

44
00:03:05,557 --> 00:03:07,148
mean stronger inhibition.

45
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:13,413
Here is the actual equilibrium. The bars show exact numerators, all divided by two hundred

46
00:03:13,413 --> 00:03:19,227
and ninety-two. Every bar is positive, so the support really contains all eleven neurons.

47
00:03:19,227 --> 00:03:22,101
The unequal heights are part of the pattern.

48
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:28,148
Check neuron zero. Its stronger inhibitors are neurons five and eight, with numerators

49
00:03:28,148 --> 00:03:34,060
thirty-seven and ten. The other ten rates have total numerator three hundred and thirteen.

50
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:39,708
Start with weak inhibition from all ten, then add the extra inhibition from those two.

51
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:45,680
The input becomes one minus three quarters times three hundred and sixty over two hundred

52
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:51,071
and ninety-two. That equals twenty-two over two hundred and ninety-two, exactly neuron

53
00:03:51,071 --> 00:03:55,460
zero's rate. The certificate verifies the same balance for all eleven.

54
00:03:55,833 --> 00:04:00,659
A matrix is just a rectangular table that records a linear calculation.

55
00:04:00,659 --> 00:04:05,620
For this network, collect the equilibrium equations and multiply by four.

56
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:11,193
The diagonal entries become four. An arrow gives three; a missing arrow gives six.

57
00:04:11,567 --> 00:04:17,299
Reading one row means multiplying its entries by the corresponding rates and adding.

58
00:04:17,299 --> 00:04:21,667
The whole table times the rate list must give four in every row.

59
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:25,967
Our exact rate list passes this test, with no decimal rounding.

60
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:32,902
Balance alone is not enough. A pencil balanced on its tip is at rest, but a nudge grows.

61
00:04:32,902 --> 00:04:36,485
Here we examine deviations from the equilibrium.

62
00:04:36,485 --> 00:04:43,054
Since every rate stays positive nearby, the threshold does not interfere with this local

63
00:04:43,054 --> 00:04:43,949
calculation.

64
00:04:44,333 --> 00:04:49,853
For one changing number, a negative multiplier in its rate-of-change equation pulls it

65
00:04:49,853 --> 00:04:54,218
back toward zero. In several dimensions a deviation can also rotate.

66
00:04:54,218 --> 00:04:58,133
What matters is whether the rotating motion shrinks or grows.

67
00:04:58,533 --> 00:05:01,999
Eigenvalues describe these basic modes of change.

68
00:05:01,999 --> 00:05:05,817
A complex pair combines rotation with growth or decay.

69
00:05:05,817 --> 00:05:11,970
Its real part controls the envelope. Here the deviation equation uses minus one quarter

70
00:05:11,970 --> 00:05:13,101
of the matrix K.

71
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,892
So we need every eigenvalue of K to lie to the right of zero.

72
00:05:17,892 --> 00:05:24,084
Numerically they do. The closest real part is about zero point zero three seven seven.

73
00:05:24,084 --> 00:05:28,548
That thin margin warns us that a picture alone is not a proof.

74
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:31,992
The exact test works with a polynomial instead.

75
00:05:31,992 --> 00:05:37,785
Even for a quadratic, coefficients can tell you whether all roots lie on the stable side.

76
00:05:37,785 --> 00:05:43,317
The Hurwitz criterion generalises this idea using determinants, precise numbers built

77
00:05:43,317 --> 00:05:44,749
from the coefficients.

78
00:05:45,133 --> 00:05:50,744
For this degree-eleven polynomial, all eleven required Hurwitz determinants are positive.

79
00:05:50,744 --> 00:05:56,355
The first three are forty-four, seven thousand five hundred and fourteen, and one million

80
00:05:56,355 --> 00:06:00,327
eight hundred and sixty-seven thousand nine hundred and twenty.

81
00:06:00,327 --> 00:06:03,037
Exact arithmetic certifies local stability.

82
00:06:03,433 --> 00:06:09,292
One more technical issue matters. The conjecture assumes certain equation systems have

83
00:06:09,292 --> 00:06:15,354
no accidental zero determinants. A determinant tells us whether a linear system collapses

84
00:06:15,354 --> 00:06:19,441
a direction. Nonzero means its square table can be inverted.

85
00:06:19,833 --> 00:06:23,295
At these settings there is a neat whole-number argument.

86
00:06:23,295 --> 00:06:26,262
Look only at remainders after division by three.

87
00:06:26,262 --> 00:06:29,476
Four leaves remainder one. Three and six leave zero.

88
00:06:29,476 --> 00:06:32,937
So every such network matrix becomes the identity table.

89
00:06:33,333 --> 00:06:39,451
The identity has determinant one. Therefore each principal determinant of the original

90
00:06:39,451 --> 00:06:43,363
integer table has remainder one, and cannot equal zero.

91
00:06:43,363 --> 00:06:47,916
For the other required determinants, replace one column by ones.

92
00:06:47,916 --> 00:06:51,045
The same remainder argument still gives one.

93
00:06:51,433 --> 00:06:56,864
This works for every graph at the standard parameters, not just eleven neurons.

94
00:06:56,864 --> 00:06:59,546
It proves nondegeneracy, not stability.

95
00:06:59,546 --> 00:07:05,320
Stability still needs the positive equilibrium and the separate exact Hurwitz checks

96
00:07:05,320 --> 00:07:06,145
we just saw.

97
00:07:06,533 --> 00:07:13,312
How small can a counterexample be? For this particular graph, every proper induced nonclique

98
00:07:13,312 --> 00:07:19,207
subgraph fails. That means keep a subset of neurons and all arrows between them.

99
00:07:19,207 --> 00:07:23,333
It does not mean every smaller graph in the world fails.

100
00:07:23,733 --> 00:07:29,603
A separate exhaustive enumeration covers all seven-neuron directed graph shapes, over

101
00:07:29,603 --> 00:07:33,540
eight hundred and eighty-two million, plus smaller sizes.

102
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:37,062
Certified rejection bounds remove impossible cases.

103
00:07:37,062 --> 00:07:43,070
All thirty-five thousand eight hundred and forty-two saved seven-neuron candidates fail

104
00:07:43,070 --> 00:07:44,589
exact stability tests.

105
00:07:44,967 --> 00:07:51,183
The result brackets the smallest possible nonclique support between eight and eleven

106
00:07:51,183 --> 00:07:55,328
neurons. Eight, nine and ten remain unresolved globally.

107
00:07:55,328 --> 00:08:01,767
Eleven is minimal under vertex deletion, but it is not proved to be the global minimum.

108
00:08:02,167 --> 00:08:07,232
A numerical trajectory shows one small perturbation returning to the actual equilibrium.

109
00:08:07,232 --> 00:08:11,261
This illustrates the behaviour, while the algebra proves local return.

110
00:08:11,261 --> 00:08:16,269
It does not show that every starting state returns, or certify a fixed percentage-sized

111
00:08:16,269 --> 00:08:16,615
basin.

112
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:22,316
This is not the first counterexample. Geneson disproved the general conjecture with six

113
00:08:22,316 --> 00:08:27,509
neurons at different settings. The public repository hturcotte1 slash C L T N already

114
00:08:27,509 --> 00:08:33,008
implied counterexamples at the standard settings, including one with twenty-three neurons.

115
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:38,324
Those repository results were present in commit three seven four four two zero zero,

116
00:08:38,324 --> 00:08:41,255
dated the fifteenth of October, twenty twenty-six.

117
00:08:41,255 --> 00:08:46,473
Our eleven-neuron graph came from shrinking its twenty-three-neuron example and modifying

118
00:08:46,473 --> 00:08:50,752
arrows. Its parameter map also already implied the small-order exclusion.

119
00:08:51,133 --> 00:08:57,432
The retained contribution is this smaller explicit witness, exact checks of its vertex-minimality,

120
00:08:57,432 --> 00:09:02,446
the universal remainder argument, and an independently certified size bracket.

121
00:09:02,446 --> 00:09:08,166
Remember: equilibrium means balanced rates; stability means local return; the full active

122
00:09:08,166 --> 00:09:09,901
group need not be a clique.

123
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:16,006
The companion paper and certificates provide the complete graph, rational rates and stability

124
00:09:16,006 --> 00:09:20,239
calculations. This film develops their mechanism and proof structure.

125
00:09:20,239 --> 00:09:25,393
The exact minimum at the standard parameters is still an open question between eight

126
00:09:25,393 --> 00:09:26,068
and eleven.
